Z||000001||mbramble||01-01-2001||10:13 AM||||The easiest way is to find a donor car with the power options you want and remove the doors and wiring harnesses from it to swap ino your car.  I did this not too long ago and it wasn't terribly difficult.  Hard part is finding the doors at a price you are willing to pay.<P>Ogre has a good writup in his Cave (link at bottom of the page) that tells what is involved.  <P>The doors I got had power locks, windows, and mirrors but the most useful of those is definitely the windows.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||01-01-2001||03:05 PM||||there are kits made to convert them, ive installed a lot of them but not in a fiero. Auto parts store or JC Whitney have them. Prob be best though if you find a junkyard one with working ones. chk em with a battery before u take them out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-01-2001||03:19 PM||||Michigan Fiero club website has everything you need to know on it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||01-02-2001||02:42 PM||||I picked up a set of power everything doors at the first swap meet at Ed Parks' Fiero Factory for $40.00.  With all the travelling I do I still haven't made the swap.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-02-2001||02:54 PM||||That's the same place I bought my pw doors. Bought them from mrfiero. I installed the pw motors along with pl and pm onto my factory doors. <BR>The way the mill and drill process was designed, virtually no two Fiero doors are drilled alike. Each are drilled specifically for their own space frame. That is why some Fiero doors just will not work on another Fiero. Plus, it wasn't that hard to transfer everything over.||||reg||1
